---
// The substrate's status as two ruled sheets: the captured test run (the readout, then its facts as a
// label column and a value column, stacked when narrow, after "Key Value List" by corr (21st.dev),
// rewritten in plain markup and CSS with none of its code or dependencies) and the brick board. The
// numbers, dates and states are read from the ledger projection; none is typed here. The styles are
// in styles/status.css and styles/readout.css.
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
import { Aside } from '@astrojs/starlight/components';
import BrickBoard from '../components/BrickBoard.astro';
import SubstrateReadout from '../components/landing/SubstrateReadout.astro';
import ledger from '../data/ledger.public.json';
import { withBase } from '../lib/url.ts';
import { formatDay } from '../lib/plan.ts';
import { stateLabel, type PublicLedger } from '../lib/ledger.ts';
import { substrateReadout } from '../lib/status.ts';
import '../styles/docs-visuals.css';
import '../styles/status.css';

const led = ledger as unknown as PublicLedger;
const t = led.tests;
const substrate = led.program.substrate;
const maps = led.program.cognitive_map;
const since = formatDay(substrate.since);
const capturedOn = formatDay(t.captured_at.slice(0, 10));
const meaningBar = stateLabel(String(led.meaning_bar.status).toLowerCase());

// Every number in the readout is read from the ledger projection.
const stats = substrateReadout(led);
const overview = withBase('substrate/overview/');
const architecture = withBase('substrate/architecture/');
const methodology = withBase('substrate/methodology/');
---

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: 'Substrate status',
    description: 'The substrate, stage 1 of Psyche: its captured test run, the build status of each brick, what its tests show, and where its development stands.',
    tableOfContents: false,
  }}
>
  <div class="sx-wide sx-status">
    <section class="sx-section" aria-labelledby="sx-status-run">
      <p class="sx-kicker"><span class="sx-kicker__index">01</span> Test run</p>
      <h2 id="sx-status-run">The captured test run</h2>
      <SubstrateReadout items={stats} framed>
        <dl class="sx-facts">
          <div class="sx-facts__row">
            <dt class="sx-facts__label">Captured</dt>
            <dd class="sx-facts__value">{capturedOn}, CPython {t.captured_python}</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="sx-facts__row">
            <dt class="sx-facts__label">Counts</dt>
            <dd class="sx-facts__value">Identical on every version listed</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="sx-facts__row">
            <dt class="sx-facts__label">Determinism</dt>
            <dd class="sx-facts__value">Probed under each hash seed</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="sx-facts__row">
            <dt class="sx-facts__label">Meaning bar</dt>
            <dd class="sx-facts__value">{meaningBar}</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </SubstrateReadout>
    </section>

    <Aside type="note" title={`${stateLabel(substrate.state)} and maintained`}>
      The substrate is Psyche's stage 1 and its foundation. Its development has been {substrate.state} since {since} (decision {substrate.decision}), while stage 2, spiking cognitive maps, is {maps.state}. No new work starts on the substrate; its tests and checks stay maintained.
    </Aside>

    <p>
      The substrate is a dependency-free Python memory-and-activation substrate. What it shows is that its lower
      layers are correct and deterministic. It makes no claim that any layer reproduces a cognitive effect.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three attempts to grade a cognitive effect were stopped before any graded run, each at a structurally
      distinct wall: semantic priming was circular, the fan effect found the mechanism absent, and interoception
      found the signal flat. The finding is scoped to this substrate and these three yardsticks. The recurring
      obstacle, an external yardstick that the project did not author, is the lesson stage 2 is built around. Its
      behavioural paradigms are published, and its baselines are reproduced against their published behaviour;
      the agent-side operationalisation and the pass criteria are the project's own and are pre-registered
      privately.
    </p>

    <section class="sx-section" aria-labelledby="sx-status-bricks">
      <p class="sx-kicker"><span class="sx-kicker__index">02</span> Build status</p>
      <h2 id="sx-status-bricks">Bricks</h2>
      <p>
        Each brick of the substrate with its build status, as the ledger records it, and its meaning verdict. The two
        are separate axes: a brick can be built and verified while no meaning verdict has been earned.
      </p>
      <p>
        Brick names such as &ldquo;the depression path&rdquo; name mechanisms in the substrate, not clinical models.
      </p>
      <BrickBoard />
    </section>

    <p>
      Read more: <a href={overview}>The substrate</a>, <a href={architecture}>Architecture</a> and <a href={methodology}>Methodology</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</StarlightPage>
